    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite is green (10/10).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all **400 / 409 / 429** rejection responses to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status` and `detail` (status codes unchanged). Every such rejection in this app flows through an escalation handler, so I converted the ten that weren't already producing problem detail, reusing the existing `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper:\n\n- **400**: `DisposableEmailDomain`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `InvalidTelephoneLength`, `MissingRequiredFields` \u2014 were returning `ResponseEntity<String>` / a custom body.\n- **409**: `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity` \u2014 were plain-string bodies.\n- **429**: `DailyOwnerLimit` \u2014 was a plain-string body.\n\nEach now builds a `ProblemDetail` with the exception message as `detail` and sends it via `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` at the same status.\n\nStructural cleanups so logic stayed where it belongs:\n- `ProblemDetails.build` was silently stamping a validation-specific `schemaValidationErrors: []` onto *every* problem. I moved that concern into `ValidationExceptionHandler` (which now always sets it), leaving the shared helper to produce only the genuinely common RFC7807 members \u2014 so it doesn't leak validation semantics into conflict/rate-limit responses.\n- `MissingRequiredFieldsExceptionHandler` preserves the offending field names as a `fields` property on the problem body, so no information was lost. The now-unused `RequiredFieldsErrorResponse` DTO was deleted.\n\n`NotFoundExceptionHandler` (404) and the 403/404/500 handlers were left as-is \u2014 404 is out of scope and the others already emitted problem detail.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreRejectionIsProblemJson`, asserting `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400` for a missing required field) and all other tests pass.",
